  ```


- IMPLEMENTED: opencvjs-primitive-vectors: Primitive type vectors API.
  **Status:** IMPLEMENTED ✓ (2026-08-13)
  - File: js_primitivevectors.cpp
  - All 5 types implemented: IntVector, FloatVector, DoubleVector, CharVector, StringVector
  - All methods working: push_back, get, set, size, delete, Symbol.iterator
  - Uses JSVector<T> template class with JSConverter specializations for primitives
  
  **opencv.js API:**
  - **IntVector** (std::vector<int>)
  - **FloatVector** (std::vector<float>)
  - **DoubleVector** (std::vector<double>)
  - **CharVector** (std::vector<char>)
  - **StringVector** (std::vector<std::string>)
  
  **API for all:**
  - Constructor: `new cv.IntVector()` / `new cv.FloatVector()` / etc.
  - Methods: `push_back(value)`, `get(i)`, `set(i, value)`, `size()`, `delete()`
  - Iterator: `for (const value of vector) { ... }`
  - Used by: Various OpenCV functions that take arrays of primitives
  
  **Example usage:**
  ```javascript
  const indices = new cv.IntVector();
  indices.push_back(0);
  indices.push_back(1);
  indices.push_back(2);
  
  const weights = new cv.FloatVector();
  weights.push_back(0.5);
  weights.push_back(0.3);
  weights.push_back(0.2);
  ```


- IMPLEMENTED: opencvjs-vector-template-infrastructure: Common template infrastructure.
  **Status:** IMPLEMENTED ✓ (2026-08-13)
  - Header: include/js_vector.hpp
  - Template class: `JSVector<T>` with common implementation
  - Template helper: `js_register_vector<T>()` for registration
  - JSConverter<T> specializations for type conversion
  
  **Design:**
  - JSVector<T> template class with all methods (push_back, get, set, size, delete)
  - js_register_vector<T>() for registration and export
  - JSConverter specializations for: int, float, double, char, std::string, cv::Mat,
    cv::Point, cv::Rect, cv::KeyPoint, cv::DMatch (DMatch pending js_dmatch.cpp)
  - Symbol.iterator support for all vector types
  - GC-friendly: finalizer handles cleanup, .delete() is optional
  
  **Files:**
  - include/js_vector.hpp - Template infrastructure
  - js_matvector.cpp - MatVector implementation
  - js_pointvector.cpp - PointVector implementation
  - js_rectvector.cpp - RectVector implementation
  - js_keypointvector.cpp - KeyPointVector implementation
  - js_dmatchvector.cpp - DMatchVector implementation
  - js_primitivevectors.cpp - Int/Float/Double/Char/String vectors


- PARTIALLY FIXED: boxpoints-only-accepts-mat: cv.boxPoints(rotatedRect, dst)
  requires `dst` to be a cv.Mat or (as of the PointVector special-case below)
  a cv.PointVector - a cv.Contour destination still throws (does not crash)
  an OpenCV assertion. Root cause is inherent to cv::boxPoints() itself, not
  this binding: it writes a raw 4x2 single-channel CV_32F matrix (matching
  opencv.js/cv2's ndarray-shaped return value), not 4 points - and a
  std::vector<Point>-backed OutputArray (what a Contour/PointVector's
  destination becomes) can only be created as an Nx1 or 1xN point-shaped
  array, never a genuine 4x2 matrix. This is not new: the previous
  JSContourData<double>-based implementation required argv[1] to be a
  cv.Contour and would have hit the same cv::_OutputArray::create() shape
  assertion when boxPoints tried to create its 4x2 result into that Contour's
  vector storage.

    import('opencv').then(cv => {
      const rr = new cv.RotatedRect({x: 50, y: 50}, {width: 40, height: 20}, 0);

      const pts = new cv.Mat();
      cv.boxPoints(rr, pts); // OK - 4x2 CV_32F Mat

      const pv = new cv.PointVector();
      cv.boxPoints(rr, pv); // OK - 4-element PointVector (see fix below)

      const ptsContour = new cv.Contour();
      cv.boxPoints(rr, ptsContour);
      // Assertion failed: d <= 2 && (size0 == 1 || size1 == 1 || size0*size1 == 0)
      //   in function 'create'
    });

  SHAPE_BOX_POINTS (js_imgproc.cpp) now special-cases a PointVector
  destination: it detects one via JSVector<cv::Point>::fromJS(argv[1]) (the
  ctx-less overload, so a non-PointVector argv[1] doesn't leave a stray
  pending exception the way the ctx-taking overload does - see FIXED:
  contourarea-spurious-internal-exception), computes boxPoints() into a local
  cv::Mat as before, and copies the resulting 4 float points across into the
  PointVector (rounded to int, matching PointVector's cv::Point element
  type). A cv.Contour destination isn't special-cased the same way (its
  points are double-precision, not int) and still hits the assertion above.

- js-typedarray-from-vector-iterator-mismatch: `js_typedarray<T>::from_vector()`
  (`include/js_typed_array.hpp`) forwards to `from_sequence(ctx, in.begin(),
  in.end())`, which calls `js_typedarray_remain()` on those iterators -
  but `js_typedarray_remain()` is only SFINAE-enabled for raw-pointer
  iterators (`std::enable_if<std::is_pointer<Iterator>::value>`).
  `std::vector<T>::begin()`/`end()` return `__gnu_cxx::__normal_iterator`
  (libstdc++'s wrapper type), not a raw `T*`, so `from_vector()` fails to
  compile for any real `std::vector<T>` - `no matching function for call
  to 'js_typedarray_remain(...)'`. Found while adding a TypedArray-receiver
  branch to `JSOutputArgument<T>` (`include/js_inputoutputarray.hpp`) and
  reaching for `from_vector()` as the obvious entry point - worked around
  there by calling `from_sequence(ctx, vec.data(), vec.data() +
  vec.size())` directly (raw pointers, so `is_pointer` SFINAE succeeds)
  instead of fixing `js_typedarray_remain()`'s SFINAE to also accept
  non-pointer random-access iterators, since that's shared machinery used
  elsewhere in this header and touching it was out of scope for that task.

    #include "include/js_typed_array.hpp"
    // T = float, JSContext* ctx already available
    std::vector<float> vec = {1.5f, 2.5f, 3.5f};
    JSValue ta = js_typedarray<float>::from_vector(ctx, vec);
    // error: no matching function for call to
    // 'js_typedarray_remain(const __gnu_cxx::__normal_iterator<...>&, ...)'

- minmaxloc-argv-out-of-bounds-read: `js_cv.cpp`'s `OTHER_MIN_MAX_LOC`
  case (backing `cv.minMaxLoc()`) supports 4 optional trailing callback
  arguments (`(minVal) => ..., (maxVal) => ..., (minLoc) => ..., (maxLoc)
  => ...`) by unconditionally reading `argv[1]` through `argv[4]` and
  checking `js_is_function()` on each - with no `argc` bound check at all:

    for(size_t i = 0; i < 4; i++)
      if(js_is_function(ctx, argv[i + 1]))
        JS_Call(ctx, argv[i + 1], JS_NULL, 1, &results[i]);

  QuickJS only guarantees `argv[]` has as many live slots as the
  function's declared `.length` (2 for `minMaxLoc`, padded with
  `undefined` up to that point) - anything beyond that is simply not part
  of the call's argv array. Every ordinary call site in this codebase
  (`cv.minMaxLoc(dst)`, `cv.minMaxLoc(dst, mask)`) reads past the end of
  that array on `argc < 5`, interpreting whatever bytes happen to follow
  as a `JSValue` and potentially dereferencing them as a heap object
  pointer inside `JS_IsFunction`. This is memory corruption, not merely a
  wrong-value bug - found because it crashed `tests/unittests/
  test_template_matching.js` with a `SIGSEGV` deep inside an *unrelated*
  later `cv.*` call (`js_cv_other()`, `argc` corrupted to `80`), i.e. the
  corruption doesn't necessarily crash at its own call site, just
  eventually. Reproducible standalone, though non-deterministically -
  crashed reliably at a fixed point in one script, silently succeeded
  6/6 and even 20/20 iterations in others depending on incidental
  heap/stack layout:

    import * as cv from 'opencv';
    for (let i = 0; i < 6; i++) {
      const dst = new cv.Mat(); // ... populated via matchTemplate or otherwise
      cv.minMaxLoc(dst); // argc=1 here - argv[2..4] read out of bounds every time
    }
    // crashes unpredictably, sometimes several iterations in, sometimes not at all

  Fixed by guarding each read with `argc > int(i) + 1` before touching
  `argv[i + 1]`. This blocked writing `test_template_matching.js` (every
  test there calls `matchTemplate` immediately followed by `minMaxLoc`),
  so it was fixed as part of that work rather than only logged - not
  otherwise in scope for the matchTemplate/TermCriteria task that surfaced
  it.

