- NOT-A-BUG: opencvjs-fs-createdatafile-not-applicable:
  `cv.FS_createDataFile(...)` is Emscripten's virtual in-memory filesystem
  API (`FS.createDataFile`), used by the 9 DNN example pages purely to
  stage a downloaded model blob where the wasm module's `cv.readNet()` can
  see it, since a browser has no real filesystem. qjs-opencv runs on a
  real filesystem (`qjsm`, native `std`/file I/O) - `cv.readNet()` (or
  whatever qjs-opencv's dnn model loader is called; see the dnn survey
  entries elsewhere in this file for the state of DNN bindings) can read
  model files directly, no staging step needed. Not a binding gap - the 9
  DNN example pages need this call deleted, not replicated, when adapted.

- uncaught-cv-exception-aborts-process-in-unguarded-modules: Most js_*.cpp
  files wrap their OpenCV calls in `try { ... } catch(const cv::Exception&
  e) { JS_ThrowInternalError(ctx, ...); }` so a bad argument turns into a
  catchable JS exception. 24 files have zero such try/catch anywhere,
  including several that call OpenCV functions with real precondition
  asserts: `js_calib3d.cpp`, `js_bg_subtractor.cpp`, `js_white_balancer.cpp`,
  `js_fast_line_detector.cpp`, `js_clahe.cpp`, `js_subdiv2d.cpp`. When one
  of those asserts fires, `cv::Exception` propagates as an uncaught C++
  exception straight through the QuickJS C call frame - `std::terminate()`
  aborts the whole process (SIGABRT), not a catchable `try/catch` in JS.
  Reproduced concretely with `js_white_balancer.cpp`'s `balanceWhite`
  (`GrayworldWB` requires `CV_8UC3`/`CV_16UC3`, has no channel check of its
  own in this binding) - a 4-channel Mat crashes the whole `qjsm` process
  instead of throwing:

    import * as cv from 'opencv';
    let src = new cv.Mat(50, 50, cv.CV_8UC4, new Uint8Array(50*50*4).fill(128));
    let dst = new cv.Mat();
    let wb = cv.createGrayworldWB();
    try {
      wb.balanceWhite(src, dst);
    } catch(e) {
      console.log("threw:", e.message); // never reached - process aborts first
    }

  Fix is mechanical per file: wrap the call dispatch in the same
  try/catch-and-JS_ThrowInternalError pattern already used by the other 68
  occurrences across `js_cv.cpp`, `js_imgproc.cpp`, `js_photo.cpp`, etc.

- typedarraytype-flags-sets-signed-bit-for-float-arrays: `TypedArrayType::flags()`
  (`include/js_typed_array.hpp`) always OR's in `TYPEDARRAY_SIGNED` (0x40)
  whenever `is_signed` is true - but `js_typedarray_type()`'s string parser
  initializes `is_signed = true` and never clears it for floating-point
  types. So a `Float32Array`/`Float64Array` gets `flags() ==
  FLOATING_POINT|SIGNED|BITS_n`, which never matches the `TYPEDARRAY_FLOAT32`/
  `TYPEDARRAY_FLOAT64` case labels (`FLOATING_POINT|BITS_n`, without SIGNED)
  in `js_typedarray_inputoutputarray`'s switch. Falls to `default:`, which
  calls `JS_ThrowTypeError(ctx, "Expected TypedArray %s", ...)`.

  That exception is never checked by the caller: `js_cv_output_argument()`
  (`include/js_inputoutputarray.hpp`) calls `js_typedarray_inputoutputarray()`
  and returns its result unchecked, and `js_cv_other()` (`js_cv.cpp`) runs
  `js_cv_inputoutputarray(ctx, argv[0])` unconditionally in its prologue for
  *every* magic case, even ones (like `OTHER_SCALAR`) that never use the
  result. The pending exception silently sits on the context and resurfaces
  later, misattributed to whatever native call frame is still active by the
  time something (module teardown, next uncaught-exception check) reports it
  - so it doesn't show up at the `cv.*()` call site itself, only after,
  confusingly attributed to an unrelated-looking line. Affects any
  `js_cv_other`-dispatched function given a Float32Array/Float64Array as an
  argument that participates in the InputOutputArray prologue.

  Fixed: `js_typedarray_type(const std::string&)` now clears `is_signed`
  when `is_floating_point` is true, matching the other `TypedArrayType`
  constructors (`mattype_signed()` already excludes `CV_32F`/`CV_64F`).
  Verified `cv.sum(new Float64Array(...))` now resolves the array instead
  of falling through to `noArray()`, and the stray exception no longer
  appears. All `tests/unittests/*.js` still pass.

    import * as cv from 'opencv';
    let r = cv.Scalar(new Float64Array([1.5, 2.5, 3.5, 4.5]));
    console.log('ok', r); // prints fine
    // ... but the script later reports an uncaught exception anyway:
    // TypeError: Expected TypedArray Float64Array

- FIXED: std-file-methods-broken-after-opencv-import: When both `std` and
  `opencv` were imported in the same `qjsm` script, `std.open()`'s returned
  object ended up with the WRONG prototype entirely - not merely missing
  methods, but literally aliased to one of opencv's own classes:

    qjsm> std.open('blah', 'a').__proto__ == opencv.Line.prototype
    true

  Root cause: a `JSClassID` collision between two *independent* copies of
  the QuickJS core that used to coexist in one process, not a bug in this
  project's C++.
  - `qjsm` statically embedded its own private QuickJS core. Confirmed via
    `nm $(which qjsm) | grep -w 'JS_NewClassID\|js_class_id_alloc'` - both
    were local/non-exported symbols (`T`/`d`) baked directly into the qjsm
    executable, and `nm -D $(which qjsm) | grep JS_` matched nothing (no
    `JS_*` symbol was in its dynamic symbol table at all). This private copy
    owned the class-id counter used when `std` registered its own `FILE`
    class.
  - `opencv.so` instead dynamically links the separately-installed
    `libquickjs.so.1.3` (`ldd build/.../opencv.so`), which has its own,
    completely independent `JS_NewClassID` and class-id counter, disconnected
    from qjsm's private one.

  Both counters start from the same baseline (`JS_CLASS_INIT_COUNT`) and
  increment independently, so a class ID `opencv.so` allocates for one of
  its own classes (here, `cv.Line`) could numerically collide with an ID
  qjsm's own private counter already handed out for `std`'s `FILE` class.
  Since there is only one `JSRuntime` in the process and `class_array` is a
  property of that shared runtime (indexed by the numeric class ID, not
  scoped per compiled copy of quickjs.c), the later registration silently
  overwrote the earlier class's slot - including its `.prototype` pointer.

  Fixed (2026-08-27) by redeploying `/usr/local/bin/qjsm` linked against the
  same `libquickjs.so` `opencv.so` is built against: `ldd $(which qjsm)` now
  shows `libquickjs.so.1.3`, and `nm -D $(which qjsm) | grep -c JS_` is 119
  instead of 0 - qjsm now shares quickjs's class-id counter with opencv.so
  instead of embedding a private copy. Verified:
  `std.open(...).__proto__ == cv.Line.prototype` is now `false`, and both
  `std.open(...).puts()` and `std.popen(...).getline()` work correctly with
  `opencv` imported. `examples/render_font.js`'s fd-based (`os.*`)
  workarounds for this have been reverted back to plain
  `std.open`/`std.popen`.

    import * as std from 'std';
    import * as cv from 'opencv';
    let f = std.open('out.json', 'w+');
    console.log(typeof f.puts); // now 'function' (was 'undefined')
    f.puts('{}'); // works
    console.log(std.open('blah', 'a').__proto__ == cv.Line.prototype); // now false (was true)

- FIXED: js_color_read-drops-short-arrays: `js_color_read(JSContext*, JSValueConst,
  JSColorData<double>*)` (`src/jsbindings.cpp:23`) used to read a JS array
  color as `v[0..3] = JS_GetPropertyUint32(color, 0..3)` and only accept the
  result if `v[0]`, `v[1]`, AND `v[2]` were all numbers; otherwise it fell
  through with `ret = 0` and left `c` at its all-zero default
  (`{0, 0, 0, 255}`), silently discarding whatever *was* actually passed -
  including `v[0]`, which was a real number. So any color array shorter than
  3 elements (e.g. `[255]` for a grayscale draw, or `[255, 0]`) read as
  opaque black instead of using the given component(s) and defaulting the
  rest. Fixed by routing through `js_iterable_to(ctx, color, out->arr)`
  first (which fills only as many components as are present, leaving the
  rest at their existing default) before falling back to the old
  property-based read. Verified:

    import { CV_8UC1, Mat, Point, rectangle } from 'opencv';
    const m = Mat.zeros(4, 4, CV_8UC1);
    rectangle(m, new Point(0, 0), new Point(3, 3), [255], -1);
    console.log(m.data[0]); // 255 (was 0 before the fix)

- cmake-libcamera-forces-cxx17: `cmake/libcamera.cmake:6` does
  `set(CMAKE_CXX_STANDARD 17)` unconditionally (whenever `USE_LCCV`/libcamera
  support is configured in - the CLAUDE.md-documented default), which makes
  CMake append `-std=gnu++17` to every compile command, after the project's
  own `-std=c++2a` from `cmake/CheckFlags.cmake`. Since gcc/clang take the
  last `-std=` flag on the command line, the effective standard for the
  whole project is C++17, not C++2a/C++20 as `CLAUDE.md` documents
  ("C++ standard is -std=c++2a") and as `-DCXX_STANDARD=2a` misleadingly
  suggests. Confirmed via `compile_commands.json` for
  `build/x86_64-linux-gnu`: the generated command contains both
  `-std=c++2a ... -std=gnu++17`, and `<ranges>`/`std::ranges::subrange`
  (C++20-only) fail to compile with `'ranges' in namespace 'std' does not
  name a type` when actually built through this build tree, despite
  compiling fine standalone with an explicit `-std=c++2a`. Not investigated
  for a fix (unclear whether libcamera's own headers actually require C++17,
  in which case this is an intentional-but-undocumented downgrade rather
  than a bug proper).

    grep -o -- '-std=[a-z+0-9]*' build/x86_64-linux-gnu/compile_commands.json | sort -u
    # -std=c++2a
    # -std=gnu++17

- FIXED: js_iterable_to-array-overload-uncallable: The `js_iterable_to` overload
  used to take its array parameter by value, `js_iterable_to(JSContext*,
  JSValueConst, T[N] out)`. A by-value array parameter decays to a pointer
  at the parameter-list level before template argument deduction runs, so
  the actual deduced signature was `js_iterable_to(JSContext*, JSValueConst,
  T*)` - `N` never appeared in it and could never be deduced from a call.
  It also called `js_iterable<T>::to_array` with a pointer where that
  function takes `std::array<T, N>&` by reference - a type mismatch that
  would have failed to compile had the overload ever been reachable. Now
  takes the parameter by reference-to-array, `T (&out)[N]` (matching the
  pattern `JSConstructor`'s constructor already used for the same reason),
  and dereferences the reinterpret_cast before passing it to `to_array`:

    template<class T, size_t N>
    static inline int64_t
    js_iterable_to(JSContext* ctx, JSValueConst arr, T (&out)[N]) {
      return js_iterable<T>::to_array(ctx, arr, *reinterpret_cast<std::array<T, N>*>(&out[0]));
    }

  `N` now deduces correctly from a real call. Not independently verified
  whether anything actually calls this overload in practice (vs. the
  sibling `std::array<T, N>&` overload) - see TODO.md's jsbindings.hpp dead
  code audit.

- clang-thread-local-nontrivial-dtor-inputoutputarray: FIXED. Was:
  `include/js_inputoutputarray.hpp:61`'s `static thread_local
  JSInputArraySlot slots[JS_INPUTARRAY_SLOTS];` (where `JSInputArraySlot`
  holds a `std::vector<double> vec` member, i.e. a non-trivial destructor)
  failed to compile under Clang 22 with "error: type of thread-local
  variable has non-trivial destruction". Root cause (confirmed via `-E`):
  `include/util.hpp:31` had `#ifndef thread_local ... #define thread_local
  __thread` (a C-only TLS-portability shim, meant for pre-C11 compilers
  lacking the keyword). In C++, `thread_local` is a language keyword, not
  a macro - `#ifndef thread_local` is unconditionally true regardless of
  standard version, so this branch fired on every C++ compile (both GCC
  and Clang) and silently rewrote every `thread_local X` in the codebase
  to `__thread X`. `__thread` is the older GNU TLS extension and can't
  carry a non-trivial constructor/destructor the way C++11 `thread_local`
  can; GCC tolerates it anyway via a documented C++-mode extension, but
  Clang correctly rejects it for a non-trivial type - which is what its
  "use 'thread_local' to allow this" note was actually asking for, once
  you know the compiler was really seeing `__thread`. `util.hpp` is a
  `.hpp` that unconditionally includes C++-only headers
  (`<opencv2/core/mat.hpp>`, `<iostream>`, ...), so it was never
  reachable as valid C in the first place - the shim had no legitimate
  case to cover in this file. Fix: wrapped the shim in `#ifndef
  __cplusplus`. Verified: Clang now compiles
  `include/js_inputoutputarray.hpp` cleanly (both standalone and via
  `-E`, confirming `thread_local` is no longer rewritten), the full
  project still builds clean under GCC, and
  `tests/unittests/test_contour_functions.js` passes at runtime.

- wunused-function-cant-see-header-only-static-inline: Neither GCC's nor
  Clang's `-Wunused-function` can find dead `static inline` functions in a
  header like `include/jsbindings.hpp` (every one of its 82 free functions
  is `static inline`; it has zero plain-`static` ones - confirmed via
  `grep -nE '^static [A-Za-z_<:].*$' include/jsbindings.hpp | grep -v
  'static inline'`, no matches). Two independent, compiler-verified
  reasons:
    1. GCC never warns for `static inline`, only plain `static` - confirmed
       on g++ 13.3.0 and 16.0.1 (trunk): `static inline int f(){}` vs
       `static int g(){}`, neither called, only `g` warns (with or without
       `-fsyntax-only`).
    2. Clang *does* warn for an unused `static inline` function, but only
       when it's declared directly in the main translation unit being
       compiled - never for one reached via `#include`, regardless of
       inline-ness otherwise. Confirmed with a 2x2 repro (static /
       static-inline x header / main-file): header-declared `static`
       warns, header-declared `static inline` doesn't; main-file-declared
       `static` and `static inline` both warn. An earlier note in this
       file claimed "Clang's -Wunused-function does fire for static
       inline (verified)" - that verification was against a main-file-only
       repro and doesn't hold for the header-inclusion case that actually
       matters for `jsbindings.hpp`, corrected here.
  Net effect: an aggregated `-Wunused-function` sweep across all 54 `.cpp`
  files that include `jsbindings.hpp` (now working end-to-end after
  clang-thread-local-nontrivial-dtor-inputoutputarray was fixed) ran
  clean under Clang with zero compile errors, but also produced zero
  findings - not because nothing is dead, but because nothing in the
  header was ever eligible to be flagged in the first place. Confirmed
  the flag genuinely fires in this exact build's command line (not
  silently swallowed by some other flag) by injecting a deliberately
  unused `static inline` canary via `-include`: still silent, consistent
  with reason 2 above (force-included files behave like `#include`, not
  like main-file content). This closes off `-Wunused-function` entirely
  as a technique for this header, under either compiler.

- FIXED: opencv-freetype-empty-bbox-garbage-metrics: `cv::freetype::FreeType2::getTextSize()`
  (upstream `opencv_contrib/modules/freetype/src/freetype.cpp`, reached
  via this project's plain passthrough at `js_draw.cpp:639` -
  `freetype2->getTextSize(text, font_scale, thickness, &baseline)`, no
  logic of this project's own involved) returns nonsensical height/baseline
  (`height=-33554431`, `baseline=-33554432` - suspicious round hex values
  `0xFE000001`/`0xFE000000`, i.e. `-(1<<25)`-ish, consistent with a
  sign/shift bug reading a glyph bbox) whenever the rendered string's ink
  bounding box is empty. Confirmed two distinct triggers, both upstream:

  1. Any single whitespace glyph (e.g. `' '`/U+20) in an otherwise-normal
     *scalable* font (verified with `/usr/share/fonts/truetype/dejavu/
     DejaVuSansMono.ttf` @ 16px) - only that glyph is garbage; `width` is
     still correct, and other glyphs ('A', '!', 'g', '.') measure fine.
     This is NOT bitmap-strike-specific and will hit essentially any font
     sheet, since space/U+20 is almost always in range.
  2. A font with embedded fixed-size bitmap strikes (e.g. an X11 bitmap
     font wrapped in TTF) at a size that *exactly* matches one of the
     font's own strikes - here EVERY glyph tested (space through U+FFFD,
     including plainly-inked ones like 'A') comes back garbage, not just
     whitespace. Requesting a *non*-matching size correctly throws a clean
     assertion instead (`!FT_Set_Pixel_Sizes(mFace, _fontHeight,
     _fontHeight)`), so this looks like a separate bug in the exact-match
     bitmap-strike code path, not simply "this glyph has no ink." **This
     makes case 2 universal for bitmap-strike fonts, not a corner case**:
     every size either (a) exactly matches a strike and hits this bug, or
     (b) doesn't match and throws the `FT_Set_Pixel_Sizes` assertion
     instead - there is no size at which `getTextSize()` returns a sane
     result for a pure bitmap-strike font. Confirmed independently across
     three different local bitmap fonts, each at its own correct native
     size (`fc-query`'s `pixelsize`):

       /home/roman/.fonts/7x13.ttf  @ 13px -> width=8  height=-33554431 baseline=-33554432
       /home/roman/.fonts/9x18.ttf  @ 18px -> width=10 height=-33554431 baseline=-33554432
       /home/roman/.fonts/7x14.ttf  @ 14px -> width=8  height=-33554431 baseline=-33554432

     Practical impact (before the fix below): `examples/render_font.js`
     could not render ANY pure bitmap-strike font end-to-end at all - the
     "every glyph bogus" guard always fired for such a font, regardless of
     `--size`.

  Fixed (2026-08-28) in this project's own binding, `js_get_text_size`
  (`js_draw.cpp`) - not in the vendored library itself (still not
  investigated/fixed there, and no local source checkout to patch, see
  `/opt/opencv-4.7.0-x86_64`). When `freetype2->getTextSize()` returns a
  value outside a generous sane range (`height < 0 || height > 100000 ||
  baseline < -100000 || baseline > 100000`), height is substituted with
  the requested pixel size and baseline with 0. This is exact, not a
  guess, for case 2: a bitmap strike's line height *is* by definition the
  pixel size it was matched at, and its glyphs are pre-composed bitmaps
  with the baseline baked into their fixed cell position - there's no
  separate ascent/descent split to recover by measurement. For case 1 (a
  genuinely empty glyph like a space in a scalable font) the same
  substitution is a safe, harmless default: something with zero ink draws
  nothing regardless of what size is reported for it. `size.width` was
  never affected by this bug and is left as returned. Verified: all three
  bitmap fonts above now return sane values (e.g. `7x13.ttf` @ 13px ->
  `8, 13, 0`), and `render_font.js` no longer throws before rendering a
  bitmap font. **However**, actually rendering one end-to-end still
  doesn't work - fixing this bug only stopped the pipeline from crashing
  before drawing; it exposed a second, separate, deeper bug in the actual
  glyph rasterization - see `opencv-freetype-bitmap-strike-glyphs-not-rasterized`.

    import { TextStyle } from './js/cvHighGUI.js';
    // case 1: scalable font, single whitespace glyph
    let style = new TextStyle('/usr/share/fonts/truetype/dejavu/DejaVuSansMono.ttf', 16);
    let baseline;
    console.log(style.size(' ', y => (baseline = y)), baseline); // height/baseline garbage; 'A' etc. are fine

    // case 2: bitmap-strike font at its own exact native size - ALL glyphs garbage
    style = new TextStyle('/home/roman/.fonts/7x13.ttf', 13);
    console.log(style.size('A', y => (baseline = y)), baseline);
    // 8 -33554431 -33554432 - expected something like 8 9 2 (or similar sane values)

- FIXED: imread-png-ignores-flags-always-rgba: `imread(filename, flags)` (`js_cv.cpp:101-124`)
  used to dispatch every `.png` file to this project's own `png_read()`
  helper (`include/png_read.hpp`) instead of `cv::imread`, and that helper
  completely ignored the `flags` argument - it hardcodes
  `png::image<png::rgba_pixel>` (via pngpp) and always builds a `CV_8UC4`
  Mat, regardless of whether the caller asked for `IMREAD_GRAYSCALE` (0),
  `IMREAD_COLOR` (1), or `IMREAD_UNCHANGED` (-1), and regardless of the
  PNG's own actual color type (a genuinely 8-bit grayscale/`L`-mode PNG,
  verified independently correct on disk via PIL, still came back
  4-channel). Found while trying to round-trip a rendered CV_8UC1 Mat
  through `imwrite`/`imread` for forensic pixel analysis -
  `imread('grayscale.png', 0).channels()` was silently 4, and reading its
  `.data` as if it were 1-channel produced nonsensical values (BGRA bytes
  interpreted as consecutive grayscale samples). Fixed by only taking the
  `png_read()` fast path when `flags == cv::IMREAD_UNCHANGED` (the one flag
  that actually asks to keep the file's alpha/original channel layout
  unchanged) - every other flag (`IMREAD_GRAYSCALE`, `IMREAD_COLOR`,
  `IMREAD_ANYCOLOR`, `IMREAD_REDUCED_*`, ...) now goes through
  `cv::imread(filename, flags)`, whose own PNG codec honors them correctly.
  Verified:

    import { CV_8UC1, IMREAD_GRAYSCALE, IMREAD_COLOR, IMREAD_UNCHANGED, Mat, imread, imwrite } from 'opencv';
    const m = Mat.zeros(4, 4, CV_8UC1);
    imwrite('gray.png', m);
    console.log(imread('gray.png', IMREAD_GRAYSCALE).channels()); // 1 (was 4)
    console.log(imread('gray.png', IMREAD_COLOR).channels());     // 3 (was 4)
    console.log(imread('gray.png', IMREAD_UNCHANGED).channels()); // 4 - unchanged, as requested

- FIXED: opencv-freetype-bitmap-strike-glyphs-not-rasterized: `cv::freetype::FreeType2::putText()`
  (upstream `opencv_contrib/modules/freetype/src/freetype.cpp`, reached via
  this project's plain passthrough at `js_draw.cpp:592` -
  `freetype2->putText(*dst, text, point, font_scale, ...)`, no logic of
  this project's own involved) does not actually rasterize the real glyph
  bitmap for a font with embedded fixed-size bitmap strikes (confirmed with
  three different local bitmap fonts: `7x13.ttf`, `9x18.ttf`, `7x14.ttf`,
  all at their own correct native pixel size). Instead of the real glyph
  shape, every character - regardless of which one, including wide ones
  like 'M'/'W'/'@' - draws only a thin 1-pixel-wide vertical sliver in the
  leftmost column of the glyph's cell, covering roughly the top 2/3 of the
  cell height and nothing else. Dumped as ASCII art (`#`=255, `+`=mid,
  `.`=faint, space=0) for 'M' on `7x13.ttf` @ 13px in an 8x13 cell:

    +
    #
    #
    #
    #
    +
    +
    +
    +
    (columns 1-7 are entirely blank; should be a full 7-wide 'M' glyph)

  Distinct from, and found while re-verifying the fix for,
  `opencv-freetype-empty-bbox-garbage-metrics` (getTextSize returning
  garbage for bitmap-strike fonts) - that bug is now fixed (substituting
  sane height/baseline values in `js_get_text_size`), which finally made it
  possible to render a bitmap font through `examples/render_font.js`
  end-to-end without throwing - but doing so revealed the output image is
  visually wrong: every glyph cell contains only this same degenerate
  sliver, not real character shapes. getTextSize returning sane numbers
  just meant the pipeline no longer crashed; the actual pixels `putText()`
  drew for such a font remained wrong. Not fixable from this project's own
  source by patching the vendored `libopencv_freetype` itself (no local
  source checkout available - it's an installed prebuilt library, see
  `/opt/opencv-4.7.0-x86_64`).

  Fixed (2026-08-28) by bypassing `cv::freetype::FreeType2` entirely for
  bitmap-strike fonts and blitting their glyphs via raw FreeType2 API calls
  instead - `include/js_freetype_bitmap.hpp` (`FreeTypeBitmapFont`), wired
  into both `js_put_text` and `js_get_text_size` (`js_draw.cpp`) behind a
  new `HAVE_RAW_FREETYPE2` build flag (`CMakeLists.txt`: `find_package(Freetype)`,
  only attempted when `OPENCV_FREETYPE_LIBRARY` is already set). Scalable
  fonts are untouched - they still go through `cv::freetype::FreeType2` as
  before, which rasterizes them correctly. For a bitmap-strike font,
  `FreeTypeBitmapFont` loads the same font file a second time through raw
  FreeType2, decodes the UTF-8 string itself, and for each codepoint calls
  `FT_Load_Glyph(..., FT_LOAD_RENDER)` and blits the real
  `FT_GlyphSlot::bitmap` (handling both `FT_PIXEL_MODE_MONO` and
  `FT_PIXEL_MODE_GRAY`) directly into the destination `cv::Mat`, alpha-blended
  per channel by coverage. `getTextSize` for a bitmap-strike font now
  returns REAL per-glyph metrics (`bitmap_top` for ascent,
  `bitmap.rows - bitmap_top` for descent) instead of the
  empty-bbox-garbage-metrics fix's substituted `(fontSize, 0)` - strictly
  more accurate. Verified against all three local bitmap fonts end-to-end
  through `examples/render_font.js`: real, correct glyph shapes, proper
  baseline alignment for ascenders/descenders, `FT_Set_Pixel_Sizes` still
  throws a clear error for a non-matching size. No regression on scalable
  fonts or the contour/psimpl test suites.

    import { TextStyle } from './js/cvHighGUI.js';
    import { CV_8UC1, LINE_AA, Mat, Point } from 'opencv';
    const style = new TextStyle('/home/roman/.fonts/7x13.ttf', 13);
    const mat = Mat.zeros(13, 8, CV_8UC1);
    style.draw(mat, 'M', new Point(0, 0), 255, -1, LINE_AA);
    console.log([...mat.data].filter(v => v != 0).length); // now 26, a real 'M' (was 9 - a sliver, not a glyph)

- freetype-puttext-y-ignores-gettextsize-ascent: For a *scalable* (non
  bitmap-strike) font, `cv::freetype::FreeType2::putText()`'s `point.y`
  does not mean "top of the ink bbox that `getTextSize()`'s reported
  ascent/height describes", contrary to this project's own doc comments
  (`examples/render_font.js`'s "Draw.text's point is the glyph box's
  top-left corner, not its baseline"). Found while building a small
  single-string terminal preview renderer in `examples/render_font.js`
  sized tightly to `ascent+descent` from `TextStyle.size()` - it rendered
  entirely blank for characters that are provably non-empty (verified they
  draw fine on a taller canvas).

  Measured the actual offset between `point.y` and where ink actually
  lands (draw at `point=(0, py)` on a generously tall canvas, scan for the
  ink row range, subtract `py`) across 3 fonts x 4 sizes:

    monogram.ttf        8px: ink at point+[4,7]     14px: point+[7,13]    24px: point+[13,23]   40px: point+[22,39]
    Pixeletter.ttf       8px: ink at point+[1,7]      14px: point+[3,13]    24px: point+[5,23]    40px: point+[8,39]
    DejaVuSansMono.ttf   8px: ink at point+[2,7]      14px: point+[4,13]    24px: point+[6,23]    40px: point+[11,39]

  In every case the ink's top edge lands roughly `0.15-0.6 * fontSize`
  *below* `point.y` (never above), and its bottom edge lands almost
  exactly at `point.y + fontSize - 1` - i.e. `point.y` behaves like "top of
  a full em-square line box", not "top of the measured glyph ink" the way
  `size()`'s `ascent` (`height` field) would imply if you tried to size a
  canvas as `ascent + descent` and draw at `y = ascent - glyphAscent` the
  way this project's own `render_font.js` grid exporter does. `size()`'s
  `.width` is unaffected - only vertical placement is wrong relative to
  what `.height`/the baseline callback claim.

  **Confirmed knock-on bug in the existing PNG/JSON grid exporter**
  (`examples/render_font.js`'s `exportFontSheet`, unchanged by this
  session, predates it): every glyph placed in **row 0** of the grid (the
  common case - it's whichever codepoints sort first, typically space and
  punctuation) silently renders nothing, because `y = row*cellHeight +
  (ascent - glyphAscent)` puts row-0 glyphs right at the canvas top with no
  headroom for this offset, and the top of a `Mat.zeros()` canvas simply
  clips ink that would land above y=0 if this offset were even larger, or
  in row 0's case, renders correctly-if-you-are-lucky since the offset here
  is instead well *below* row 0's y - meaning the row-0 glyph is drawn far
  enough down that it bleeds into row 1's cell rather than clipping, and
  what actually shows up empty is row 0's expected cell position, not row 1
  itself. Verified via `qjsm examples/render_font.js monogram.ttf
  --end=0x7f` then checking pixel data at the JSON-reported cell for a
  row-0 character (`!`, U+21): zero ink in that cell despite `!` being a
  perfectly ordinary non-empty glyph.

  Worked around (not fixed - this is upstream, same file/line as the two
  bugs above) in the new terminal preview renderer added this session
  (`TerminalGlyphRenderer` in `examples/render_font.js`): draws into a
  generously tall scratch canvas (`fontSize*2 + 4`) at `point.y=0`, then
  scans for the actual ink row range and crops to that before rendering -
  sidesteps trusting `point.y` placement math entirely, at the cost of a
  bit of wasted canvas per render. The grid exporter's row-0 bug is left
  as-is per this project's "surgical changes" convention (not what this
  session was asked to fix); worth doing the same generous-canvas-then-crop
  treatment there if row-0 export fidelity ever matters.

    import { TextStyle } from './js/cvHighGUI.js';
    import { CV_8UC1, LINE_AA, Mat, Point } from 'opencv';
    const style = new TextStyle('/home/roman/Downloads/fonts/monogram.ttf', 16);
    const mat = Mat.zeros(8, 7, CV_8UC1); // sized to size('A').height (8) - looks reasonable, isn't
    style.draw(mat, 'A', new Point(0, 0), 255, -1, LINE_AA);
    console.log([...mat.data].filter(v => v != 0).length); // 0 - nothing drawn, despite 'A' being non-empty

- render_font-single-strike-assumption: `render_font.js`'s `queryFontMetadata()`
  only captures the FIRST `pixelsize` value fc-query prints
  (`/^pixelsize=(\d+...)/m`, no `/g`), and `findCrispSize()`/the detail
  view built on it accordingly only ever report ONE embedded-bitmap-strike
  size as "the" crisp size. But the bitmap-strike table formats
  (`EBLC`/`CBLC`/older `bloc`) are explicitly designed to hold a `strikes`
  array - a single font FILE can legitimately bundle several distinct
  fixed pixel sizes (e.g. 8/10/12/16px), each a genuine 1-bit/grayscale
  bitmap, not a scaled copy. Confirmed the tables/API exist and are
  queryable via fontTools on this machine (`NotoColorEmoji.ttf`'s `CBLC`
  table, `TTFont(f)['CBLC'].strikes[i].bitmapSizeTable.ppemX/ppemY`) -
  just didn't happen to find a genuinely multi-strike (>1 entry) file
  installed locally to verify the multi-size case end-to-end against.
  Not fixed this session (discovered while answering a direct question,
  out of scope for the color-mode work in progress) - `queryFontProgramInfo()`
  (already shells out to fontTools for other program-level facts) is the
  natural place to add an `EBLC`/`CBLC`/`bloc` strikes list, and
  `findCrispSize()` would need to report/offer all of them rather than
  assuming one.

    python3 -c "
    from fontTools.ttLib import TTFont
    t = TTFont('/usr/share/fonts/truetype/noto/NotoColorEmoji.ttf', lazy=True)
    for strike in t['CBLC'].strikes:
        h = strike.bitmapSizeTable
        print(h.ppemX, h.ppemY, h.bitDepth)
    "
    # -> one strike here (109 109 32) - single-strike on this machine, but
    # the `strikes` list itself is exactly where a multi-strike font's
    # sizes would show up if one were installed.

